os closer to real-world  application and leveraging the platform  features to manage and track experiment  parameter sweeps and model snapshots  how does it work  every time we execute flute we are  launching a job that consists of one or  more independent nodes that can be  physical or virtual machines that can  execute optical workers which are  independent os level processes  a working can be either a client or a  server during each iteration round the  orchestrator which is the worker serial  dispatches the central model to the rest  of the workers and then queue a subject  line tasks for workers to execute  the workers receive that line task which  is basically the training data plus the  configuration of the task and execute  stochastic gradient descent on the  central model using that line training  date  once this is done the clients send those  audio gradients back to the orchestrator  where they will be aggregated  flute uses the library of message  passing interface well known as openmpi  for its communication backbone which is  defined in federated.pi  once the server is instantiated it acts  as the orchestrator which takes care of  the following  run an initial validation testing round  if the options have been enabled in the  configuration file  set the initial learning rate for the  workers  sample deadlines from the pool of  available workers dispatching training  tests for all the sampled lines and  received back the solo gradients  note that at this point we already have  the cellular gradients for each line and  they come with a specific weight that  can vary accordingly if we are using the  ea which is dynamic reading aggregation  or fed average  perform aggregation  and finally some round stat and profi  and profiling steps  let's move on to client all of the  processes to compute the gradients that  are delivered to the server happen here  each independent line performs the  following  whereas the configuration test prepares  the line data that will be sampled for  training  initializes the model optimizer data  loaders and the scheduler  performs several iterations of the  training on the client's training data  estimates the federated optimizer  weights such as the ea or fed average  and finally prepares smaller parameters  to be transmitted back to the server  how do i build my federated land  solution using flute  during the following section we are  going to explain what are the steps to  follow in order to create our federated  land solution with flute  first we need to incorporate our machine  learning model previously frame it in  python  at leaning the standard declaration of  the machine learning model requires the  following five functions inside the  platform  first the constructor with the model  definition and their respective layers  second the loss function that computes  the loss that we are using during the  training rounds  third  the inference function that is used to  perform the metrics used during  evaluation rounds and finally the sets  function that will bring the model into  evaluation or training mode  the example shown in the screen was  taken from the cnn classifier experiment  in  flute second data pre-processing at the  moment flute supports hdf5 and json  formatted inputs and requires a specific  format for the training data which is a  dictionary of the following  users an array arrival with a unique id  for each user id in the training data  user data a dictionary containing the  user index training data in the form of  a dictionary  number of samples and array with the  number of samples for each user in the  same order of the user list  user data label this can be optional a  dictionary with the same structure as  user data containing the training data  labels in the form of a dictionary  the format displayed on the screen is  mandatory because of the design of loot  but we provide some sample scripts for  pre-processing the data into this format  in utils pre-processing folder  the easiest way to start experimenting  with the different features that flute  provides is strokes during the  configuration file while all the setup  for the task are started  flute config files have the following  sections  first modal config contains the path and  parameters if are needed to initialize  the model  second tpconfig  contains a differential privacy setup  and can be disabled  third privacy metrics config this is for  cached data to compute additional  metrics can be disabled as well  fourth strategy for choosing the  federated optimizer we want to use  either gta or fed average for instance  server config determines all the server  side settings  and finally client config dictates the  learning parameters for client-side  model updates  in order to see which parameters can be  used for each section you can find fully  documented examples inside the configs  folder  now that we have covered the basics for  incorporating your machine learning  model solution into affiliate learning  framework let's move to the demo  during this last section we will show  you step by step how to run one of the  tasks reconfigured in flute and we will  demonstrate how each step described in  the previous section was incorporated  for this task  hi there  in this tutorial you will learn how to  run your first federated experiment in  flute  for this you're going to need 3 things  installed in your computer git anaconda  and some implementation of mpi  we have tested both mpi for windows on  windows and openmpi on linux  during this tutorial i'll first show you  how to clone the flute repository and  how to install the required python  packages  next we'll take a closer look at one of  the experiments we have implemented on  the repository where we train the  classification model on the cipher10  dataset  you will see how model data and  parameters are set for this experiment  and how to actually run it in your  computer  with this are you ready to start  this 